## Answer

[RUN] The declared target is weakly learnable as an ordering signal, but not as a useful
held-out level or top-1 predictor with the declared C1 interface. The sealed `(8,)` head's
leave-one-anchor-out (LOAO) ordering was `0.5889`, above the constant predictor's `0.5`,
and its 2,000-replicate anchor bootstrap interval was `[0.5658, 0.6115]`. Its raw held-out
level `R²` was `−0.2724` (bootstrap interval `[−0.5750, −0.0435]`) and top-1 accuracy was
`0.1711`, indistinguishable in practical terms from the constant floor `0.1716`.

[INFERRED] Widening does not rescue the head. A flexible nonlinear function of the same
16 slots raises LOAO `R²` to only `0.0933`, ordering to `0.6197`, and top-1 to `0.1854`,
then loses level transfer under leave-one-world-out (LOWO: `R² = −0.0714`). Therefore the
dominant observed ceiling looks informational. There is also an architectural/optimization
penalty—the flexible fit is better than the sealed head in level `R²`—but it is not the
load-bearing explanation because widths 32 and 128 do not improve reliably and the best
flexible fit remains weak. This is empirical evidence, not a proof of the Bayes ceiling.

[INFERRED] The answer to “learnable at all?” is consequently: **a little for pairwise
ordering, no persuasive evidence for held-out level calibration or correct argmax choice.**
That is not strong enough to support a load-bearing C1 selector conclusion.

## Evidence labels and scope

- `[RUN]`: verified by running the specified interpreter against the current workspace.
- `[DERIVED]`: follows algebraically from the declared definitions.
- `[INFERRED]`: interpretation of the run/derived evidence.

[RUN] This diagnostic ran no production-pipeline training, policy, acceptance test, or EE
endpoint. Loss values are not used as EE evidence. Nothing here makes an EE claim.

